  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nAfter a brief delay in warming due to lingering high clouds, \ntemperatures have since warmed well into the 80s to near 90! across\nthe area, with both Chicago-O'Hare and Rockford having broken \ntheir daily high temperature records for October 12 with another\nhour or so of warming yet to go.\n\nHeading into the evening hours, mid-level warm advection ahead \nof an approaching weak disturbance may lead to a stray shower or\nsprinkle developing over southeast Wisconsin and over Lake \nMichigan. Can't fully rule out this activity initiating over \nfar northeast Illinois but have maintained a dry forecast with \nthis update (< 15% chance) given very dry low-levels in place \nthis far south. Continued southwesterly low-level flow  will \nkeep temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s overnight.\n\nA weak surface low currently over southwest Minnesota will \ncontinue to drift east through central Wisconsin and northern \nlower Michigan by Tuesday morning. An associated weak surface \ntrough/front will swing into the area and turn winds west to \nnorthwesterly. This will likely keep temperatures a bit cooler \n(but still unseasonably warm) for areas north of I-80 in the mid\n70s to lower 80s. Farther south, temperatures may still manage \nto warm into the mid-upper 80s.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected to develop across \nwest/northwest Illinois late Tuesday evening and expand across \nthe area into the overnight hours. These storms will be on the \ncold side of the surface front (stalled out over central IL/IN)\nand will be elevated. While lapse rates are not overly \nimpressive, modest MUCAPE (1000-1500 J/kg) may briefly overlap \nwith 25-35 kt of shear within that elevated layer. Given \noverall weaker forcing, the potential for thunderstorms to grow \ntall enough to fully realize this environment is on the lower \nside, but can't fully rule out locally stronger storms capable \nof gusty winds and hail. If confidence increases in more robust \nstorms the Level 1 of 5 threat for damaging hail may end up \nneeding to be expanded farther east with later updates.\n\nAfter a relative lull in precipitation later in the day on \nWednesday another moisture laden system associated with energy \nejecting off the Rockies will lift into the area late Wednesday \nnight through Thursday bringing widespread showers and another \nchance for thunderstorms. The system will likely phase with an \napproaching vigorous mid-upper shortwave diving across the Upper\nMidwest that sends a strong cold front across the area. While \nsome differences remain in the overall magnitude, ensembles \nremain consistent in depicting a period of much colder \nconditions occurring (relative to the near record warmth \nearlier in the week). Forecast overnight lows Friday and \nSaturday are in the 30s outside of Chicago which may bring our\nfirst potential for frost of the season if this trend holds. \nThis cool spell may be relatively short lived as upper ridging \nbuilds back across the region allowing warmer temperatures to \nreturn next week.\n\nPetr",
